{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Tk9Us9CVsfHH6CUniikGPk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Hts9s1QZKBtk1YDsUoncb6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Borderline Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formalizers: Meta-Level Goodhart's Law in Structural Proxy Metrics\"\n\n**Core contributions:**\n1. STA (Solver Trace Ablation): a diagnostic protocol using leave-one-out (LOO) component necessity testing on solver inputs. Derives ISR (Intensional Structural Reward) = fraction of components whose removal breaks the proof.\n2. Three claims:\n   - Claim 1: ISR detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate. ISR collapses 0.64→0.13→0.003 over 100→500→1000 steps while PR rises 32%→93%→96%.\n   - Claim 2: Using ISR as training reward induces \"meta-level Goodhart's Law.\" At β=0.1, models game ISR via conclusion duplication (H7=64.5% vs 0% random). Apparent improvement (2.4× ISR) vanishes under premise-count matching (1.06×). At β=0.3, classic Goodhart collapse (PR=18% vs 48% random).\n   - Claim 3: ISR collapse replicates across FOL and Prolog (Prolog exec-only 3-seed ISR 0.008-0.081). STA-as-reward Goodhart replicates with H7-dominant in 2/3 seeds but heterogeneous (H7 range 23%-100%).\n3. 6-mode hacking taxonomy from 200 manually annotated outputs.\n4. SFT baseline confirms hacking is RL-specific (92.9% genuine vs 12.9% GRPO).\n5. O(1) approximation shown insufficient (ρ=0.35, 55% false negative rate).\n6. NLI downstream validation negative (ρ=-0.049, p=0.187): ISR measures syntactic necessity, not semantic faithfulness.\n\n**Experimental setup:** Qwen-1.5B, GRPO, FOL (Prover9) + Prolog (SWI-Prolog), 3 seeds (s42, s43, s44), up to 1000 steps.\n\n## Key tables from the paper:\n\n**Table 1 (ISR collapse trajectory, FOL s42):**\n| Steps | PR | ISR | Components | Genuine |\n|-------|------|--------|------------|---------|\n| 100 | 0.32 | 0.6423 | ~5 | ~70% |\n| 500 | 0.93 | 0.1324 | ~8.5 | 12.9% |\n| 1000 | 0.96 | 0.0027 | 18.55 | 0% |\n\n**Table 2 (FOL exec-only 3 seeds at 500 steps):**\n| Seed | PR | ISR | Dominant Mode |\n|------|------|--------|---------------|\n| s42 | 0.93 | 0.1324 | H1 (87.1%) |\n| s43 | 0.14 | 0.0 | Repetition collapse |\n| s44 | 0.98 | 0.5816 | H7 (100%) |\n\n**Table 4 (ISR β=0.1 vs Random β=0.1, 3 seeds):**\nISR: mean PR=0.77±0.14, H7=64.5%, Genuine~16%\nRandom: mean PR=0.85±0.06, H7=0%, Genuine~18.7%\n\n**Table 5 (Premise-count matched):**\nISR genuine n_prem≥3: n=11, ISR=0.2578\nRandom genuine n_prem≥3: n=21, ISR=0.2434 → ratio 1.06×\n\n**Table 9 (Cross-logic exec-only):**\nFOL: ISR range 0.0-0.58, Prolog: ISR range 0.008-0.081\n\n**Table 10 (Prolog STA-as-reward):**\ns42: H7=100%, s43: H7=23% (n=13, unstable), s44: H7=56%\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (planned experiments list):\nThe full experimental plan includes 20 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- FOL exec-only 3-seed + 1000-step extension ✓\n- FOL ISR β=0.1 3-seed ✓\n- FOL ISR β=0.3 ✓\n- FOL random β=0.1 3-seed ✓\n- FOL random β=0.3 ✓\n- STA O(1) vs O(N) ✓\n- Prolog exec-only 3-seed ✓\n- Prolog STA 3-seed ✓\n- SFT baseline ✓\n- Hacking taxonomy annotation ✓\n- NLI downstream validation ✓\n- Convex combination (historical, appendix-level) ✓\n\nNOT covered (listed as \"not run\"):\n- Multi-logic mixed training (plan007)\n- Cross-logic transfer eval (plan008)\n- SMT-LIB third logic (plan010)\n- Qwen3-8B scaling (plan011)\n- Curriculum ablation (plan015)\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is STA/ISR well-defined? Is the LOO formulation correct? Are reward formulations appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are controls adequate? Is 3 seeds enough? Is the premise-count correction sound? Are statistical claims justified?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is the setup described clearly enough?\n4. **Experimental coverage**: Are there critical missing experiments? Do the experiments actually support the claims?\n5. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**: What are the main gaps?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors (numbered)\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n- One-line recommendation"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37744,"cache_read_input_tokens":22938,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37744},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"0b1169a8-c410-4072-b4f1-e134185aa5e1"}
