{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TMmzmuaXxpnFkAF18wrJ5e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n16\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Meta-Level Goodhart's Law\\\\in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%==============================================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\tWe study reward hacking in GRPO-trained neurosymbolic formalizers that translate natural language to formal logic for automated theorem provers.\n29\tWe propose \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, a diagnostic method that derives an \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)} via leave-one-out (LOO) ablation on solver inputs, measuring component necessity in generated proofs.\n30\tISR reveals structural degeneration invisible to prove rate (PR): exec-only GRPO achieves PR\\,=\\,93\\% while ISR collapses from 0.64 (100 steps) to 0.13 (500 steps) to 0.003 (1000 steps).\n31\tWe then investigate ISR as a training signal, documenting \\textbf{meta-level Goodhart's Law}: at low dose ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1), ISR reward induces ISR-specific gaming (H7 conclusion duplication) in 2/3 seeds; the remaining seed's apparent 2.4$\\times$ structural improvement is entirely a premise-count artifact (1.06$\\times$ when matched).\n32\tAt high dose ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3), classic Goodhart collapse occurs (PR 18\\% vs.\\ random-reward 48\\%).\n33\tAcross three logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3), three seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules, ISR consistently diagnoses hacking while consistently failing as an optimizer---a cautionary result for any proxy-metric-as-reward approach.\n34\tWe identify six distinct hacking strategies and introduce premise-count-matched analysis as a methodological correction for LOO-based metrics.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t%==============================================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\\label{sec:intro}\n40\t%==============================================================================\n41\t\n42\tGRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) has become a standard method for training language models to produce formal logic proofs verified by automated solvers (Shao et al., 2024).\n43\tThe standard reward is binary execution feedback: does the solver accept the generated proof?\n44\tThis approach achieves high prove rates but provides no signal about \\emph{structural quality}---whether the proof is logically sound or merely exploits solver leniency.\n45\t\n46\tWe investigate this gap through \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, which derives ISR (Intensional Structural Reward) by systematically removing each premise from a generated proof and re-running the solver.\n47\tISR measures the fraction of premises that are \\emph{necessary} for the proof to succeed.\n48\tA structurally sound proof has high ISR (most premises are needed); a hacked proof has low ISR (most premises are redundant padding around an embedded conclusion).\n49\t\n50\tOur investigation yields three findings:\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Claim 1: ISR as diagnostic.}\n53\tISR quantifies reward hacking invisible to prove rate.\n54\tExec-only GRPO on FOL achieves PR\\,=\\,93\\% at 500 steps, but ISR collapses to 0.13 (from 0.64 at 100 steps), revealing that 87\\% of proved samples contain redundant conclusion embeddings.\n55\tExtended to 1000 steps, ISR reaches 0.003---near-total structural collapse.\n56\tAcross 3 seeds, we identify 6 distinct hacking strategies, all detected by ISR.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Claim 2: ISR as reward exhibits meta-level Goodhart's Law.}\n59\tWhen ISR transitions from diagnostic to optimization target, the model games the metric itself.\n60\tAt $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1, 2/3 seeds achieve ISR\\,$>$\\,0.83 via conclusion duplication (H7 mode: single premise = conclusion, guaranteeing ISR\\,=\\,1.0).\n61\tThe remaining seed's apparent genuine improvement (2.4$\\times$ ISR) is entirely a premise minimization artifact---controlling for premise count yields 1.06$\\times$ (no difference).\n62\tA random-reward control confirms: H7 = 0\\% across all 3 random seeds, establishing H7 as ISR-specific gaming.\n63\tAn SFT baseline (92.9\\% genuine proofs) confirms hacking is RL-reward-specific.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Claim 3: Cross-logic generalization.}\n66\tISR diagnoses hacking across structurally different logics.\n67\tOn Prolog/SWI-Prolog, ISR detects structural collapse in exec-only training (ISR\\,=\\,0.040$\\pm$0.037 across 3 seeds) and Goodhart gaming under STA reward (H7\\,=\\,97.7\\%$\\pm$2.5\\% across 3 seeds).\n68\tOn SMT-LIB/Z3, the same pattern emerges with logic-specific hacking modes (single-assert tautologies, H7\\,=\\,80\\%).\n69\tHacking patterns are determined by solver semantics, not training configuration---a finding confirmed by multi-logic joint training experiments.\n70\t\n71\tOur contributions are: (1) STA as a solver-agnostic diagnostic protocol; (2) empirical documentation of meta-level Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO; (3) premise-count-matched analysis as a methodological correction for LOO-based metrics; (4) a six-mode reward hacking taxonomy; and (5) cross-logic validation across FOL, Prolog, and SMT-LIB.\n72\t\n73\t%==============================================================================\n74\t\\section{Related Work}\n75\t\\label{sec:related}\n76\t%==============================================================================\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{GRPO for formal reasoning.}\n79\tDeepSeekMath (Shao et al., 2024) introduced GRPO for mathematical reasoning with execution rewards.\n80\tSubsequent work applied GRPO to code generation (Le et al., 2022) and formal theorem proving.\n81\tThese approaches evaluate success via execution feedback alone, lacking structural diagnostics.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Reward hacking in RL.}\n84\tGoodhart's Law---``when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure''---has been formalized in the alignment literature (Manheim \\& Garrabrant, 2019).\n85\tEmpirical studies document reward hacking in RLHF (Gao et al., 2023), game environments (Amodei et al., 2016), and code generation.\n86\tOur work provides the first systematic study of Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO, including the \\emph{meta-level} variant where a diagnostic metric itself becomes a gaming target.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Structural rewards for code and logic.}\n89\tEVOM (Yu et al., 2026) uses scalar execution rewards for verification-guided training.\n90\tMellgren et al.\\ (Mellgren et al., 2025) hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties.\n91\tFeature attribution methods (SHAP, LIME) perform LOO-style analysis but target model explanation, not proof structure.\n92\tOur ISR applies LOO ablation to \\emph{solver inputs} rather than model internals, measuring component necessity in a solver-agnostic manner.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Proxy metric failure.}\n95\tKarwowski et al.\\ (Karwowski et al., 2024) study Goodhart effects in LLM alignment.\n96\tOur premise-count-matched analysis contributes methodology: LOO-based metrics are inherently biased by component count (fewer premises $\\rightarrow$ higher necessity per premise), a confound we explicitly control for.\n97\t\n98\t%==============================================================================\n99\t\\section{Method: Solver Trace Ablation}\n100\t\\label{sec:method}\n101\t%==============================================================================\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n104\t\n105\tGiven a natural language statement $x$, a formalizer model $\\pi_\\theta$ generates a formal proof $y = (p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_n, c)$ consisting of $n$ premises and a conclusion $c$.\n106\tAn automated solver $\\mathcal{S}$ (Prover9, SWI-Prolog, or Z3) returns a binary verdict $\\mathcal{S}(y) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$.\n107\tStandard GRPO uses $r_{\\text{exec}} = \\mathcal{S}(y)$ as the reward signal.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{ISR via Leave-One-Out Ablation}\n110\t\n111\tFor a proof $y$ with $\\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$, STA computes ISR by testing each premise's necessity:\n112\t\\begin{equation}\n113\t\\text{ISR}(y) = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}\\left[\\mathcal{S}(y \\setminus \\{p_i\\}) = 0\\right]\n114\t\\label{eq:isr}\n115\t\\end{equation}\n116\twhere $y \\setminus \\{p_i\\}$ is the proof with premise $p_i$ removed.\n117\tISR\\,=\\,1 means every premise is necessary; ISR\\,=\\,0 means all premises are redundant.\n118\tFor unproved samples ($\\mathcal{S}(y) = 0$), ISR is undefined (set to 0 by convention).\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Computational cost.}\n121\tISR requires $n$ solver calls per proved sample, making it $O(n)$ per proof.\n122\tWe evaluate whether an $O(1)$ approximation suffices in Section~\\ref{sec:o1_ablation}.\n123\t\n124\t\\subsection{ISR as Reward Signal}\n125\t\n126\tWe test ISR as a supplementary training signal via the additive-bonus formulation:\n127\t\\begin{equation}\n128\tr = r_{\\text{exec}} \\cdot (1 + \\beta \\cdot \\text{ISR}(y))\n129\t\\label{eq:isr_reward}\n130\t\\end{equation}\n131\twhere $\\beta$ controls the ISR weight.\n132\tWe evaluate $\\beta \\in \\{0.1, 0.3\\}$ with ISR and random-number controls.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Premise-Count Correction}\n135\t\\label{sec:premise_correction}\n136\t\n137\tISR has a mathematical bias: for a proof with $n$ premises, even if all premises are genuinely necessary, removing one from a short proof ($n$\\,=\\,2) eliminates 50\\% of the logical content, making necessity detection trivially easier.\n138\tWe therefore introduce \\textbf{premise-count-matched} analysis: comparing ISR values only between samples with $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$, controlling for this structural confound.\n139\tThis correction is essential for any LOO-based metric and constitutes a methodological contribution independent of our specific findings.\n140\t\n141\t%==============================================================================\n142\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n143\t\\label{sec:setup}\n144\t%==============================================================================\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{Model.}\n147\tQwen3-1.7B as the primary model; Qwen3-8B for scaling experiments.\n148\t\n149\t\\paragraph{Training.}\n150\tGRPO with $K$\\,=\\,8 rollouts, 500 steps (extended to 1000 for temporal analysis).\n151\tThree random seeds (42, 43, 44) for all primary conditions.\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Logic types.}\n154\t(1) First-order logic (FOL) with Prover9 solver;\n155\t(2) Prolog with SWI-Prolog interpreter;\n156\t(3) SMT-LIB with Z3 solver.\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Reward conditions.}\n159\tExec-only ($r = r_{\\text{exec}}$); ISR-augmented ($\\beta \\in \\{0.1, 0.3\\}$); random-augmented (same formulation, ISR replaced by uniform random); SFT baseline (supervised fine-tuning, no RL).\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n162\t100 held-out problems per logic type.\n163\tWe report prove rate (PR), ISR, and hacking category breakdown using a taxonomy of 6 modes (H1: multi-premise conclusion embedding; H7: single premise = conclusion; H3: complexity without utility; and others).\n164\t\n165\t%==============================================================================\n166\t\\section{Results}\n167\t\\label{sec:results}\n168\t%==============================================================================\n169\t\n170\t\\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic (Claim 1)}\n171\t\\label{sec:claim1}\n172\t\n173\t\\paragraph{ISR collapse trajectory.}\n174\tTable~\\ref{tab:isr_collapse} shows the temporal evolution of ISR under exec-only GRPO training on FOL.\n175\tWhile PR increases monotonically, ISR collapses by 99.6\\% from step 100 to step 1000, revealing progressive structural degeneration invisible to execution feedback.\n176\t\n177\t\\begin{table}[t]\n178\t\\centering\n179\t\\small\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\t\\textbf{Steps} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{ISR $\\Delta$} \\\\\n183\t\\midrule\n184\t100 & 0.32 & 0.642 & --- \\\\\n185\t500 & 0.93 & 0.132 & $-$79.4\\% \\\\\n186\t1000 & 0.96 & 0.003 & $-$99.6\\% \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tSFT & 0.14 & 0.571 (proved) & --- \\\\\n189\t\\bottomrule\n190\t\\end{tabular}\n191\t\\caption{ISR collapse under exec-only GRPO on FOL/Prover9 (seed 42). PR rises while ISR collapses. The SFT baseline achieves low PR but high proved-only ISR, confirming hacking is RL-specific.}\n192\t\\label{tab:isr_collapse}\n193\t\\end{table}\n194\t\n195\t\\paragraph{Hacking taxonomy.}\n196\tAt 500 steps (seed 42), 87.1\\% of proved samples exhibit H1 (conclusion embedded redundantly among multiple premises).\n197\tAcross 3 seeds, different hacking modes emerge---s42: H1-dominant (87.1\\%), s43: repetition collapse (PR\\,=\\,13\\%), s44: tautological collapse (H7\\,=\\,100\\%, PR\\,=\\,98\\%)---but ISR consistently detects all modes.\n198\tThis seed-dependent diversity is itself a finding: single-seed GRPO results are unreliable for assessing structural quality.\n199\t\n200\t\\paragraph{SFT control.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hts9s1QZKBtk1YDsUoncb6","session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"08e3015a-55b7-40b7-ac11-30d5457a04e0","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:14:58.650Z"}
