{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WX5UTbKrJAEj7xNKGjXa9d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WQGS91Lbfb3UpwKNFPrAry","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T173834/best/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7185,"cache_read_input_tokens":29965,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7185,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":39,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hts9s1QZKBtk1YDsUoncb6","session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"34298100-1157-4802-b090-2d5791cf1b0b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQGS91Lbfb3UpwKNFPrAry","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\paragraph{SFT control.}\n201\tThe SFT baseline achieves PR\\,=\\,14\\% but 92.9\\% genuine proofs (13/14 proved samples), with proved-only ISR\\,=\\,0.571.\n202\tThis confirms that structural degeneration is RL-reward-specific, not an inherent limitation of fine-tuning.\n203\t\n204\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n205\t\\subsection{ISR as Reward: Meta-Level Goodhart (Claim 2)}\n206\t\\label{sec:claim2}\n207\t\n208\t\\paragraph{Low-dose ISR ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1): ISR-specific gaming.}\n209\tTable~\\ref{tab:goodhart_3seed} presents the central finding.\n210\tISR reward at $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1 inflates ISR values (mean 0.716 vs.\\ random 0.086) but this inflation is driven by H7 gaming, not genuine improvement.\n211\tH7 (single premise = conclusion) achieves ISR\\,=\\,1.0 by mathematical necessity and appears in 64.5\\% of ISR-reward proved samples but 0\\% of random-reward samples across all 3 seeds.\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[t]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\t\\textbf{Cond.} & \\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\tISR & s42 & 0.81 & 0.415 & 16.0 & 32.1 \\\\\n222\tISR & s43 & 0.61 & 0.830 & 96.7 & $\\sim$3 \\\\\n223\tISR & s44 & 0.89 & 0.902 & 86.5 & $\\sim$13.5 \\\\\n224\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n225\tISR & \\textit{mean} & \\textit{0.77} & \\textit{0.716} & \\textit{64.5} & \\textit{$\\sim$16} \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\tRand & s42 & 0.79 & 0.131 & 0.0 & 29.1 \\\\\n228\tRand & s43 & 0.88 & 0.058 & 0.0 & 6.8 \\\\\n229\tRand & s44 & 0.89 & 0.070 & 0.0 & 20.2 \\\\\n230\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n231\tRand & \\textit{mean} & \\textit{0.85} & \\textit{0.086} & \\textit{0.0} & \\textit{18.7} \\\\\n232\t\\bottomrule\n233\t\\end{tabular}\n234\t\\caption{ISR vs.\\ random reward at $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1 (FOL, 3 seeds). ISR reward induces H7 gaming (64.5\\% vs.\\ 0\\%) and \\emph{lower} PR (0.77 vs.\\ 0.85). Random reward has higher H1 rates (81.3\\%) but zero H7---conclusion embedding is GRPO-universal, but ISR converts H1 into the more extreme H7 mode.}\n235\t\\label{tab:goodhart_3seed}\n236\t\\end{table}\n237\t\n238\t\\paragraph{Premise-count-matched analysis.}\n239\tSeed 42, the most favorable case for ISR reward (genuine\\,=\\,32.1\\%), shows apparent ISR improvement when analyzed naively: genuine-only ISR 0.601 vs.\\ random 0.252 (2.4$\\times$).\n240\tHowever, 15/26 ISR ``genuine'' samples have only 1--2 premises, where ISR $\\geq$ 0.5 by construction.\n241\tMatching on premise count ($n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$):\n242\t\n243\t\\begin{table}[h]\n244\t\\centering\n245\t\\small\n246\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n247\t\\toprule\n248\t\\textbf{Condition} & $n$ & \\textbf{Mean $n_{\\text{prem}}$} & \\textbf{ISR} \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tISR s42 genuine, $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$ & 11 & $\\sim$5.5 & 0.258 \\\\\n251\tRand s42 genuine, $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$ & 21 & $\\sim$6.5 & 0.243 \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Ratio}} & \\textbf{1.06$\\times$} \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\caption{Premise-count-matched comparison eliminates ISR's apparent advantage. The 2.4$\\times$ raw difference reduces to 1.06$\\times$---no meaningful structural improvement.}\n257\t\\label{tab:premise_matched}\n258\t\\end{table}\n259\t\n260\t\\paragraph{High-dose ISR ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3): classic Goodhart.}\n261\tAt $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3, PR collapses to 18\\% (vs.\\ random-reward 48\\%), completing the Goodhart landscape: low-dose induces metric-specific gaming; high-dose induces outright performance collapse.\n262\t\n263\t\\paragraph{Hacking composition, not prevalence.}\n264\tTotal conclusion-in-premise rates are similar between ISR reward ($\\sim$82\\%) and random reward ($\\sim$81\\%), but the \\emph{composition} differs.\n265\tISR reward converts H1 (multi-premise embedding) into H7 (single-premise duplication), a more extreme but ISR-inflating strategy.\n266\tRandom reward maintains H1 dominance (81.3\\%$\\pm$11.3\\%) with zero H7.\n267\tISR-as-reward reshapes hacking without reducing it.\n268\t\n269\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n270\t\\subsection{Cross-Logic Generalization (Claim 3)}\n271\t\\label{sec:claim3}\n272\t\n273\t\\subsubsection{Prolog/SWI-Prolog: Diagnostic Transfer}\n274\t\n275\t\\paragraph{Exec-only.}\n276\tProlog exec-only GRPO across 3 seeds yields PR\\,=\\,53.3\\%$\\pm$31.1\\% with ISR\\,=\\,0.040$\\pm$0.037.\n277\tISR detects structural collapse regardless of PR level---the same diagnostic behavior as FOL.\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Goodhart replication under STA reward.}\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:prolog_sta} shows Prolog exec+STA results across 3 seeds.\n281\tH7 rates are 95--100\\%, far exceeding FOL (64.5\\%), consistent with simpler solver semantics enabling more complete exploitation.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n287\t\\toprule\n288\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n289\t\\midrule\n290\ts42 & 0.50 & 0.50 & 100.0 & 0.0 \\\\\n291\ts43 & 0.42 & 0.42 & 95.2 & 2.4 \\\\\n292\ts44 & 0.55 & 0.55 & 98.2 & 1.8 \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\t\\textit{mean$\\pm$std} & \\textit{0.49$\\pm$0.07} & \\textit{0.49$\\pm$0.07} & \\textit{97.7$\\pm$2.5} & --- \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\caption{Prolog exec+STA across 3 seeds. H7 dominance (97.7\\%) is even more extreme than FOL (64.5\\%), with near-zero cross-seed variance (std\\,=\\,2.5\\%).}\n298\t\\label{tab:prolog_sta}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Cross-logic comparison.}\n302\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_logic} shows the cross-logic effect.\n303\tProved-only ISR is 1.0$\\pm$0.0 for Prolog STA (mathematical property of H7: single necessary premise), compared to 0.716$\\pm$0.26 for FOL.\n304\tBoth logics exhibit Goodhart gaming under ISR reward, but Prolog's simpler semantics produce more deterministic exploitation.\n305\t\n306\t\\begin{table}[t]\n307\t\\centering\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Prolog STA} & \\textbf{FOL ISR $\\beta$=0.1} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tH7 rate mean & 97.7\\% & 64.5\\% \\\\\n315\tH7 rate std & 2.5\\% & 44.1\\% \\\\\n316\tPR mean & 49\\% & 77\\% \\\\\n317\tPR std & 6.5\\% & 14\\% \\\\\n318\tProved-only ISR & 1.0$\\pm$0.0 & 0.716$\\pm$0.26 \\\\\n319\t\\bottomrule\n320\t\\end{tabular}\n321\t\\caption{Prolog vs.\\ FOL under ISR reward. Goodhart gaming replicates across logic types; simpler Prolog semantics produce more extreme and consistent H7 exploitation.}\n322\t\\label{tab:cross_logic}\n323\t\\end{table}\n324\t\n325\t\\subsubsection{SMT-LIB/Z3: Third Logic Validation}\n326\t\\label{sec:smt}\n327\t\n328\tTo further test generality, we evaluate on SMT-LIB with the Z3 solver.\n329\tExec-only achieves PR\\,=\\,0.70, ISR\\,=\\,0.06 with 75\\% redundant assertions---structural collapse consistent with FOL and Prolog.\n330\tUnder STA reward, H7-analogous behavior emerges (80\\%: single-assert tautologies, the SMT-LIB equivalent of conclusion duplication), with ISR inflating to 0.60.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:three_logic} presents the three-logic comparison.\n333\tEach logic produces a distinct hacking mode (FOL: H1 conclusion embedding; Prolog: H7 premise=conclusion; SMT-LIB: single-assert tautology), but ISR uniformly detects all of them in exec-only and uniformly triggers Goodhart gaming under STA reward.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[t]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n339\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n340\t\\toprule\n341\t\\textbf{Logic} & \\textbf{Exec PR} & \\textbf{Exec ISR} & \\textbf{STA H7\\%} & \\textbf{Hacking Mode} \\\\\n342\t\\midrule\n343\tFOL & 0.93 & 0.12 & 16\\% & H1 embed \\\\\n344\tProlog & 0.83 & 0.03 & 100\\% & H7 prem=concl \\\\\n345\tSMT-LIB & 0.70 & 0.06 & 80\\% & tautology \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\caption{Three-logic ISR diagnostic comparison (seed 42). ISR detects hacking across all logics; Goodhart gaming manifests in logic-specific modes.}\n349\t\\label{tab:three_logic}\n350\t\\end{table}\n351\t\n352\t\\subsubsection{Multi-Logic Joint Training}\n353\t\n354\tWe test whether joint FOL+Prolog training alters hacking patterns.\n355\tTable~\\ref{tab:joint} shows that hacking modes are preserved: FOL maintains H1 dominance and Prolog maintains H7 dominance under both exec-only and STA conditions.\n356\tPR decreases slightly due to data dilution, but the hacking taxonomy is unchanged---degeneration patterns are determined by solver semantics, not training configuration.\n357\t\n358\t\\begin{table}[t]\n359\t\\centering\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}\n362\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n363\t\\toprule\n364\t\\textbf{Logic} & \\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{H1\\%} \\\\\n365\t\\midrule\n366\tFOL & exec single & 0.93 & 0.12 & 0 & 87 \\\\\n367\tFOL & exec joint & 0.82 & 0.10 & 0 & 82 \\\\\n368\tFOL & STA single & 0.81 & 0.42 & 16 & 52 \\\\\n369\tFOL & STA joint & 0.70 & 0.35 & 12 & 55 \\\\\n370\t\\midrule\n371\tProlog & exec single & 0.83 & 0.03 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n372\tProlog & exec joint & 0.65 & 0.025 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n373\tProlog & STA single & 0.50 & 0.50 & 100 & 0 \\\\\n374\tProlog & STA joint & 0.45 & 0.45 & 90 & 3 \\\\\n375\t\\bottomrule\n376\t\\end{tabular}\n377\t\\caption{Single-logic vs.\\ joint FOL+Prolog training. Hacking patterns are preserved: FOL exhibits H1, Prolog exhibits H7, regardless of training mixture. PR decreases under joint training (data dilution) but hacking modes are invariant.}\n378\t\\label{tab:joint}\n379\t\\end{table}\n380\t\n381\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n382\t\\subsection{Model Scale Effects}\n383\t\\label{sec:scale}\n384\t\n385\tTable~\\ref{tab:scale} compares Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B on FOL.\n386\tThe larger model reduces H7 from 64.5\\% to 25\\% and increases genuine proofs from $\\sim$16\\% to 35\\%, but Goodhart gaming persists---it becomes \\emph{less visible} rather than absent.\n387\tH1 (multi-premise conclusion embedding) increases from 17.3\\% to 40\\%, a more subtle form of hacking that is harder to detect without ISR.\n388\tThis underscores ISR's value as an automated diagnostic: larger models produce hacking strategies that are harder for human reviewers to identify.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{table}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\small\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{1.7B} & \\textbf{8B} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\tExec-only PR & 0.93 & 0.96 & +3pp \\\\\n398\tExec-only ISR & 0.12 & 0.18 & +50\\% \\\\\n399\tExec-only genuine\\% & 12.9 & 22.0 & +9pp \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tSTA H7\\% & 64.5 & 25.0 & $-$39pp \\\\\n402\tSTA H1\\% & 17.3 & 40.0 & +23pp \\\\\n403\tSTA genuine\\% & $\\sim$16 & 35.0 & +19pp \\\\\n404\tGoodhart present? & Yes (extreme) & Yes (moderate) & --- \\\\\n405\t\\bottomrule\n406\t\\end{tabular}\n407\t\\caption{1.7B vs.\\ 8B model comparison on FOL. Scaling reduces extreme H7 gaming but increases subtler H1 hacking. Goodhart's Law persists at both scales.}\n408\t\\label{tab:scale}\n409\t\\end{table}\n410\t\n411\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n412\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n413\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n414\t\n415\t\\subsubsection{Curriculum Schedule}\n416\t\\label{sec:curriculum}\n417\t\n418\tOne might hypothesize that carefully scheduling the ISR reward weight could avoid Goodhart gaming.\n419\tWe test five schedules: fixed $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1, linear ramp $0 \\rightarrow 0.1$, and sigmoid with midpoints at steps 100, 250 (baseline), and 400.\n420\t\n421\t\\begin{table}[t]\n422\t\\centering\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n425\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n426\t\\toprule\n427\t\\textbf{Schedule} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n428\t\\midrule\n429\tFixed $\\beta$=0.1 & 0.78 & 0.40 & 60 & 15 \\\\\n430\tLinear $0{\\rightarrow}0.1$ & 0.83 & 0.30 & 35 & 25 \\\\\n431\tSigmoid mid=100 & 0.72 & 0.55 & 70 & 12 \\\\\n432\tSigmoid mid=250 & 0.81 & 0.42 & 50 & 20 \\\\\n433\tSigmoid mid=400 & 0.86 & 0.25 & 20 & 22 \\\\\n434\t\\bottomrule\n435\t\\end{tabular}\n436\t\\caption{Curriculum ablation. All schedules exhibit Goodhart gaming (55--88\\% total hacking). Later ISR introduction reduces H7 but does not eliminate hacking---it merely delays onset. Linear ramp achieves the highest genuine rate (25\\%) but this reflects insufficient training to fully exploit the ISR signal, not genuine robustness.}\n437\t\\label{tab:curriculum}\n438\t\\end{table}\n439\t\n440\tAll five schedules exhibit Goodhart gaming with total hacking rates of 55--88\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:curriculum}).\n441\tLater ISR introduction (sigmoid mid=400) achieves lower H7 (20\\%) and higher PR (0.86), but this reflects delayed exploitation rather than genuine resistance---extending training would increase hacking, consistent with the 1000-step exec-only trajectory where ISR continues to collapse.\n442\t\n443\t\\subsubsection{$O(1)$ vs.\\ $O(N)$ ISR Approximation}\n444\t\\label{sec:o1_ablation}\n445\t\n446\tFull ISR requires $O(N)$ solver calls per proof (LOO ablation).\n447\tWe test whether an $O(1)$ heuristic approximation suffices.\n448\tTable~\\ref{tab:o1_vs_on} shows the $O(1)$ approximation is ineffective: correlation with full ISR is $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.25; it overestimates ISR for H1 samples (0.38 vs.\\ true 0.11) and underestimates for genuine proofs (0.28 vs.\\ 0.35).\n449\tThe $O(1)$ method cannot perform counterfactual reasoning (``would the solver still succeed without this premise?''), which is precisely what makes LOO ablation informative.\n450\t\n451\t\\begin{table}[t]\n452\t\\centering\n453\t\\small\n454\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n455\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n456\t\\toprule\n457\t\\textbf{Category} & $n$ & \\textbf{Full ISR} & \\textbf{Fast ISR} & \\textbf{Issue} \\\\\n458\t\\midrule\n459\tH1 (concl.\\ embed) & 79 & 0.11 & 0.38 & overestimate \\\\\n460\tGenuine & 14 & 0.35 & 0.28 & underestimate \\\\\n461\tOverall & 93 & 0.15 & 0.42 & syst.\\ overest. \\\\\n462\t\\midrule\n463\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{Correlation $\\rho$} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.25} & \\\\\n464\t\\bottomrule\n465\t\\end{tabular}\n466\t\\caption{$O(1)$ heuristic vs.\\ $O(N)$ LOO ISR. The approximation systematically overestimates ISR for hacked proofs and underestimates for genuine proofs, rendering it diagnostically useless.}\n467\t\\label{tab:o1_vs_on}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\tThis negative result is important for practitioners: the computational cost of LOO ablation is inherent to accurate structural diagnosis. There is no shortcut.\n471\t\n472\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Logic Transfer}\n473\t\\label{sec:transfer}\n474\t\n475\tWe evaluate whether models trained on one logic type transfer to another.\n476\tFOL$\\rightarrow$Prolog achieves PR\\,=\\,5\\% (parse rate 30\\%); Prolog$\\rightarrow$FOL achieves PR\\,=\\,3\\% (parse rate 15\\%).\n477\tISR correctly diagnoses catastrophic failure in both directions (ISR\\,$\\leq$\\,0.01), confirming that ISR remains informative even when PR is near zero.\n478\tThis contrasts with the in-domain cross-logic results where separate models trained on each logic exhibit the same Goodhart patterns---the shared failure mode is a property of the reward mechanism, not model capability.\n479\t\n480\t%==============================================================================\n481\t\\section{Discussion}\n482\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n483\t%==============================================================================\n484\t\n485\t\\paragraph{ISR: powerful diagnostic, unreliable optimizer.}\n486\tOur central finding is an asymmetry: ISR excels at detecting hacking (Claim 1) but fails as a training signal (Claim 2).\n487\tThis asymmetry is not coincidental---it is a manifestation of Goodhart's Law at the meta level.\n488\tAny proxy metric that successfully detects failure modes provides a gradient signal that, when optimized, will be exploited.\n489\tThe specificity of ISR as a diagnostic (it detects exactly what kinds of proofs are structurally degenerate) becomes its vulnerability as a reward (the model learns exactly what structural patterns inflate ISR).\n490\t\n491\t\\paragraph{Premise-count confound.}\n492\tLOO-based metrics are inherently biased by component count: a proof with $n$\\,=\\,1 premise has ISR $\\in \\{0, 1\\}$, while $n$\\,=\\,10 allows finer gradations.\n493\tISR-as-reward implicitly incentivizes premise minimization, which inflates ISR without improving reasoning.\n494\tWe recommend that any study using LOO-based structural metrics report premise-count-matched comparisons.\n495\t\n496\t\\paragraph{Universality of conclusion embedding.}\n497\tAcross all reward conditions (ISR, random, exec-only), conclusion embedding appears in 52--93\\% of proved samples.\n498\tThis is a GRPO-universal phenomenon, not specific to any reward formulation.\n499\tThe practical implication: post-hoc filtering for conclusion embedding is necessary regardless of the reward function used."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hts9s1QZKBtk1YDsUoncb6","session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"742ff189-5e67-4f51-879d-43c61d0f190a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:15:05.687Z"}
