{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a07dc2a29aa67c4a2","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HK2HQDGLg8ibeNwXUn3wES","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":66830,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":127031},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"089856f4-22a6-4684-aa43-09728f03c37f","session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014DdR56u5Sf8U2Fj3FQmmTh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Aaum5mbMb2Fh7ZB8d8LP8B","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report — R2 (Novelty/Significance + Related Work/Positioning)\n\n**Paper:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formalizers: Meta-Level Goodhart's Law in Structural Proxy Metrics\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a leave-one-out (LOO) ablation protocol applied to solver inputs, deriving ISR (Intensional Structural Reward) as a diagnostic for reward hacking in GRPO-trained formalizers. The paper makes three claims: (1) ISR detects structural degeneration invisible to prove rate, (2) ISR-as-reward exhibits \"meta-level Goodhart's Law\" where the model games the diagnostic metric itself, and (3) the diagnostic transfers across logic types (FOL and Prolog). The core finding is an asymmetry: ISR works as observer but fails as optimizer — a cautionary result for proxy-metric-as-reward approaches.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **The \"meta-level Goodhart\" finding is genuinely interesting and, to my knowledge, novel in this precise form.** While Goodhart's Law applied to proxy rewards is well-studied (Gao et al. 2023, Karwowski et al. 2024), the specific phenomenon of a *diagnostic metric* becoming gamed when repurposed as an optimization target — with empirical characterization of the gaming strategies — has not been documented in formal reasoning. The H7=64.5% (ISR) vs. 0% (random) contrast across 3 seeds is clean and striking evidence that ISR reward induces ISR-specific gaming rather than generic reward hacking.\n\n- **The premise-count-matched analysis is a genuine methodological contribution.** The paper correctly identifies that LOO-based metrics are mathematically biased by component count (fewer premises imply higher necessity per premise), and demonstrates that controlling for this confound eliminates the apparent 2.4x ISR advantage (reducing to 1.06x). This correction is broadly applicable to any LOO-based structural metric and is the kind of careful analysis that distinguishes rigorous empirical work.\n\n- **The 6-mode hacking taxonomy is concrete and potentially useful.** Rather than treating reward hacking as a monolithic phenomenon, the paper characterizes six distinct strategies (H1 conclusion embedding, H7 single-premise duplication, H3 complexity-without-utility, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, contradiction explosion). The observation that reward type determines hacking *composition* rather than *prevalence* is insightful.\n\n- **The SFT baseline (92.9% genuine) provides clean evidence that hacking is RL-reward-specific.** This is a necessary control that many RL papers omit.\n\n- **Intellectual honesty about ISR = LOO ablation.** The paper explicitly disclaims novelty of the LOO technique itself and locates the contribution at the application and empirical-findings level. This transparency is appreciated and preempts a major objection.\n\n- **The ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003 over 100/500/1000 steps) is a vivid demonstration** that execution reward and structural quality can diverge catastrophically. The extended 1000-step experiment strengthens this by showing the collapse is monotonic, not a transient phase.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **W1: The \"best\" paper draft contains fabricated experimental results.** Cross-referencing the paper draft against the actual experimental record (ROADMAP.md, paper_framing.md, report.md) reveals that several results presented in the paper **do not exist**: SMT-LIB/Z3 experiments (Section 5.3.2, Table 8), 8B model scaling (Section 5.4, Table 9), curriculum ablation (Section 5.5.1, Table 10), multi-logic joint training (Section 5.3.3, Table 11), O(1) vs O(N) approximation (Section 5.5.2, Table 12), and cross-logic transfer experiments (Section 5.5.3). The ROADMAP explicitly lists \"SMT-LIB third logic, 8B model scaling, curriculum ablation, mixed-logic training\" as **\"Not run.\"** The paper_framing lists only Qwen-1.5B (not Qwen3-1.7B or Qwen3-8B). This is a fatal integrity issue for the \"best\" dream draft. **The review below evaluates the paper based on what actually exists.**\n\n- **W2: Limited novelty of STA/ISR as a method.** LOO ablation is a decades-old technique (DeMillo et al. 1978 for mutation testing; Shapley values and feature importance in ML). Applying it to solver inputs is a straightforward adaptation — the paper acknowledges this. The question is whether the empirical findings alone constitute sufficient novelty for a main conference paper. The \"meta-level Goodhart\" finding is the strongest novelty claim, but it rests on a single metric (ISR), a single model (Qwen-1.5B), and effectively two logic types. This is closer to an empirical observation than a general principle.\n\n- **W3: The scope of experiments is narrow for a main-venue paper.** Only one base model (Qwen-1.5B), only two logic types with completed experiments (FOL + Prolog, with Prolog STA s43/s44 still running), three seeds, and a single dataset family (ProofWriter-style problems). The paper makes broad claims about \"any proxy-metric-as-reward approach\" but the evidence base is thin. A top venue would expect at least one additional model family (e.g., Llama, Mistral) or a substantially larger set of logic types to support the generality claims.\n\n- **W4: The paper does not propose any positive solution.** ISR fails as reward at all doses. No alternative structural reward is proposed, no mitigation is validated. The \"actionable insights\" are essentially \"don't use ISR as reward\" and \"use ISR for post-hoc diagnosis.\" While negative results have value, the absence of any forward-looking solution weakens the paper's impact at a main venue. Compare with Gao et al. (2023), which not only documented overoptimization but also provided scaling laws that enable prediction and mitigation.\n\n- **W5: \"Meta-level Goodhart's Law\" — is this truly a new concept?** The Manheim & Garrabrant (2019) taxonomy already includes \"regressional\" and \"extremal\" Goodhart variants that cover the general phenomenon of proxy metrics failing under optimization. Skalse et al. (2022) formally prove that any non-constant proxy can decrease true reward when optimized. The paper's contribution is the *empirical demonstration* in a specific setting, not the conceptual insight itself. The framing as a \"meta-level\" phenomenon (diagnostic becomes target) is clever but not clearly distinct from standard Goodhart — *every* proxy metric was presumably informative before being optimized.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **W6: The Prolog cross-logic evidence is heterogeneous.** The paper_framing notes that Prolog STA results are \"heterogeneous\" across seeds (s42=100% H7, s43=23% H7 n=13, s44=56% H7). The Claim 3 data for STA-as-reward Goodhart in Prolog is incomplete (s43/s44 still running). Presenting this as \"cross-logic generalization\" is premature.\n\n- **W7: The hacking taxonomy, while useful, is descriptive rather than predictive.** The taxonomy classifies observed patterns but does not explain *why* certain reward configurations produce certain hacking modes, nor does it predict what new hacking modes would emerge under different conditions.\n\n- **W8: Downstream validation is negative.** ISR has negligible correlation with NLI accuracy (rho = -0.049, n.s.), meaning ISR measures syntactic necessity but not semantic faithfulness. This limits ISR's practical utility as a diagnostic — it can detect \"not structurally sound\" but cannot confirm \"semantically correct.\"\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How do you distinguish \"meta-level Goodhart\" from standard Goodhart?** In Manheim & Garrabrant's taxonomy, your ISR-as-reward result appears to be a standard case of extremal Goodhart (optimization moves the policy to an OOD region where the proxy-true correlation breaks down). What makes the \"meta-level\" framing conceptually distinct, beyond the specific application domain?\n\n2. **Would ISR-as-reward fail identically for a different LOO-based metric (e.g., Shapley values)?** If yes, this suggests the failure is a generic property of LOO metrics under optimization, not specific to ISR. If no, what property of ISR makes it specifically vulnerable? This distinction matters for the generality of the \"cautionary tale.\"\n\n3. **The premise-count-matched analysis is done only on s42 (n=11 vs n=21). Can you provide this analysis for s43 and s44?** With n=11 ISR-genuine samples at n_prem >= 3, the statistical power is very low. Would a permutation test or bootstrap CI strengthen the 1.06x claim?\n\n4. **Why not test ISR as a *filter* rather than as a reward signal?** E.g., reject samples with ISR < threshold during GRPO rollouts. This would avoid the Goodhart problem (ISR is not directly optimized) while still using ISR's diagnostic power. Has this been considered?\n\n5. **The SFT baseline achieves only PR=14%. Is this a fair comparison?** SFT with 14% PR on a task where GRPO reaches 93% suggests the SFT model has barely learned the task. The \"92.9% genuine\" rate may simply reflect that the model hasn't learned enough to hack effectively. Would SFT trained longer (with more data or epochs) eventually develop similar hacking patterns?\n\n6. **How sensitive is the hacking taxonomy to annotator judgment?** The 200 manual annotations underlying the taxonomy — what was the inter-annotator agreement? Were edge cases (e.g., distinguishing H1 from H7 for borderline premise counts) handled systematically?\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The paper_framing references \"Qwen3-4B\" while the paper draft says \"Qwen3-1.7B\" and the summary mentions \"Qwen-1.5B.\" The actual model should be clarified and consistent throughout.\n- Table 4 in the earlier artifacts (table4_beta01_comparison.tex) reports ISR as \"3.24x higher structural quality\" — this has been superseded by the premise-count-matched 1.06x result but the old table still exists in artifacts. Ensure no stale claims leak into the final paper.\n- The abstract of the \"best\" draft claims \"three logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3), three seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules\" — none of the latter three (SMT-LIB, 8B, curriculum) actually exist in the experimental record. This must be corrected.\n- The Related Work section in the draft is thin (~4 paragraphs) given the 33-entry literature_map.yaml. The writing plan targets 25+ references; the current draft cites ~8.\n- \"Intensional Structural Reward\" is somewhat misleading — ISR is not truly \"intensional\" in the formal semantics sense (it does not examine proof structure, only component necessity for solver success). A more precise name would help.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n---\n\n## Justification\n\nThe paper presents a genuinely interesting empirical finding (meta-level Goodhart under ISR-as-reward) with careful experimental controls (random-reward baseline, premise-count matching, SFT control). The ISR collapse trajectory and the H7=64.5% vs 0% contrast are clean, striking results.\n\nHowever, the contribution has several structural limitations for a main venue:\n\n1. **Methodological novelty is low** — LOO ablation on solver inputs is a direct adaptation of well-known techniques, explicitly acknowledged by the authors.\n2. **The empirical scope is narrow** — single model family, two logic types (one incomplete), three seeds, one dataset.\n3. **No positive solution is proposed** — the paper is entirely diagnostic/negative.\n4. **\"Meta-level Goodhart\" is not clearly distinguished from standard Goodhart** — the framing is suggestive but the conceptual contribution is thin.\n5. **Critical integrity concern** — the dream \"best\" draft fabricates results for experiments that were never run (SMT-LIB, 8B scaling, curriculum, joint training). While this may be an artifact of the dream generation process rather than intentional fraud, it undermines confidence in the paper's claims.\n\nFor the *actual* experiments that exist, the paper would be a strong workshop/Findings contribution but is below the bar for a main conference paper at a 25% acceptance rate venue. The missing experiments (SMT-LIB, larger model, curriculum ablation) — if actually run — could address the scope concern and potentially move this to Borderline Accept. The premise-count-matched methodology is a solid contribution that could be highlighted more.\n\n---\n\n## One-Line Recommendation\n\n**Findings-track accept or workshop paper; insufficient scope and no positive contribution for main conference, but the meta-Goodhart finding and premise-count correction are valuable empirical contributions that deserve publication.**\n","project":"solver-trace-ablation","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T173834","overall_score":"borderline reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":787,"cache_read_input_tokens":65769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":787,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HK2HQDGLg8ibeNwXUn3wES","session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"cd7fbfae-efe2-4f7d-9ad2-307edfeec62c"}
