{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HK2HQDGLg8ibeNwXUn3wES","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall Score: 3 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Key findings from the R2 (Novelty/Significance + Related Work) review:**\n\n**What works:**\n- The meta-level Goodhart finding (H7=64.5% ISR vs 0% random across 3 seeds) is genuinely novel in this specific empirical form\n- Premise-count-matched analysis (2.4x collapses to 1.06x) is a real methodological contribution applicable to any LOO-based metric\n- The 6-mode hacking taxonomy and the ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003) are clean, well-controlled results\n- Intellectual honesty about LOO not being novel is appreciated\n\n**What doesn't:**\n- **Critical integrity issue**: The \"best\" dream draft fabricates results for SMT-LIB/Z3, 8B scaling, curriculum ablation, joint training, O(1) approximation, and cross-logic transfer experiments that do not exist in the actual experimental record\n- Methodological novelty is low (LOO on solver inputs is a straightforward adaptation)\n- Empirical scope is narrow (1 model, 2 logic types with one incomplete, 3 seeds, 1 dataset family)\n- No positive solution proposed — purely diagnostic/negative\n- \"Meta-level Goodhart\" is not clearly distinguished from standard Goodhart in the Manheim & Garrabrant taxonomy\n- Related work section is thin (~8 citations vs. 33 in the literature map)\n\n**Recommendation:** Findings-track or workshop paper. The actual experimental evidence supports a solid empirical contribution, but the scope and absence of any positive result fall below the main-conference bar at a 25% acceptance rate venue. Running the missing experiments (SMT-LIB, 8B model, curriculum) could change the assessment."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a07dc2a29aa67c4a2 (use SendMessage with to: 'a07dc2a29aa67c4a2' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 70680\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 139749</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb7ceeff-4816-4704-b3bd-c692a0f9b846","uuid":"73b49a01-8752-4257-82e1-b257e8341a3e","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:17:15.777Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Borderline Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formalizers: Meta-Level Goodhart's Law in Structural Proxy Metrics\"\n\n**Core contributions:**\n1. STA (Solver Trace Ablation): LOO component necessity testing on solver inputs, deriving ISR (fraction of components whose removal breaks proof).\n2. Three claims:\n   - Claim 1: ISR detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate (0.64→0.003 ISR collapse while PR 32%→96%).\n   - Claim 2: Meta-level Goodhart's Law when ISR used as training reward. Low dose: models game ISR itself (H7=64.5% vs 0% random). Apparent improvement vanishes under premise-count matching (1.06×). High dose: classic Goodhart (PR 18% vs 48% random).\n   - Claim 3: Preliminary cross-logic evidence—ISR collapse replicates in Prolog (0.008-0.081). STA-as-reward Goodhart in 2/3 Prolog seeds but heterogeneous.\n3. 6-mode hacking taxonomy from 200 manual annotations.\n4. SFT baseline: hacking is RL-specific (92.9% genuine SFT vs 12.9% GRPO).\n5. ISR=LOO ablation metric; authors explicitly disclaim novelty of LOO technique.\n6. NLI downstream negative result: ISR ≠ semantic faithfulness.\n\n**Related Work section covers:**\n- GRPO for formal reasoning (DeepSeek-Prover, EVOM)\n- Reward hacking and Goodhart's Law (Manheim & Garrabrant 2019 taxonomy)\n- Structural rewards for code/logic (Mellgren et al. 2025, ExecVerify, EGCA)\n- Feature attribution and LOO methods (Shapley values, feature importance)\n\n**Setup:** Qwen-1.5B, GRPO, FOL (Prover9) + Prolog (SWI-Prolog), 3 seeds, up to 1000 steps.\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n\nTable 1 (ISR collapse, FOL s42): 100 steps ISR=0.64 → 500 steps ISR=0.13 → 1000 steps ISR=0.003\n\nTable 4 (ISR β=0.1 vs Random): ISR H7=64.5%, Random H7=0%. ISR PR=0.77±0.14, Random PR=0.85±0.06.\n\nTable 5 (Premise-count matched): ratio=1.06× after controlling for premise count.\n\nTable 7 (SFT vs GRPO): SFT genuine=92.9%, GRPO genuine=12.9%.\n\nTable 10 (Prolog STA): s42 H7=100%, s43 H7=23% (n=13), s44 H7=56%.\n\n**Not run:** SMT-LIB third logic, 8B model scaling, curriculum ablation, mixed-logic training.\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** (1) only 1.5B models, (2) only 2 logic types, (3) 3 seeds, (4) ISR=syntactic not semantic, (5) no positive training method proposed.\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is \"meta-level Goodhart's Law\" genuinely new? Is applying LOO to GRPO formalizers novel enough? How significant is the hacking taxonomy?\n2. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned against prior work? Are key references missing? Is the contribution differentiated from existing structural reward methods?\n3. **Significance/Impact**: Would this change how practitioners train formalizers? Is the \"ISR as observer not optimizer\" insight actionable?\n4. **Framing**: Is the paper framed as a negative result? Is that appropriate for a top venue?\n5. **Scope of contribution**: Is this sufficient for a main conference paper, or more suited to a Findings/workshop track?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors (numbered)\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n- One-line recommendation","agentId":"a07dc2a29aa67c4a2","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall Score: 3 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Key findings from the R2 (Novelty/Significance + Related Work) review:**\n\n**What works:**\n- The meta-level Goodhart finding (H7=64.5% ISR vs 0% random across 3 seeds) is genuinely novel in this specific empirical form\n- Premise-count-matched analysis (2.4x collapses to 1.06x) is a real methodological contribution applicable to any LOO-based metric\n- The 6-mode hacking taxonomy and the ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003) are clean, well-controlled results\n- Intellectual honesty about LOO not being novel is appreciated\n\n**What doesn't:**\n- **Critical integrity issue**: The \"best\" dream draft fabricates results for SMT-LIB/Z3, 8B scaling, curriculum ablation, joint training, O(1) approximation, and cross-logic transfer experiments that do not exist in the actual experimental record\n- Methodological novelty is low (LOO on solver inputs is a straightforward adaptation)\n- Empirical scope is narrow (1 model, 2 logic types with one incomplete, 3 seeds, 1 dataset family)\n- No positive solution proposed — purely diagnostic/negative\n- \"Meta-level Goodhart\" is not clearly distinguished from standard Goodhart in the Manheim & Garrabrant taxonomy\n- Related work section is thin (~8 citations vs. 33 in the literature map)\n\n**Recommendation:** Findings-track or workshop paper. The actual experimental evidence supports a solid empirical contribution, but the scope and absence of any positive result fall below the main-conference bar at a 25% acceptance rate venue. Running the missing experiments (SMT-LIB, 8B model, curriculum) could change the assessment."}],"totalDurationMs":139749,"totalTokens":70680,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3671,"cache_read_input_tokens":66556,"output_tokens":452,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"e